# The script of every c-testsuite test (msil_ctest_format.py yields one lit
# test per entry of libc-free.txt and libc.txt, 'MSIL ::
# Programs/c-testsuite/<name>' and 'MSIL :: Programs/c-testsuite/libc/<name>',
# and lit runs this file as if it were the test's own). The format gives the
# test four substitutions: %{msil_ctest_name} (the name), %{msil_ctest_tree}
# (the cached tree, c-testsuite-<ref>), %{msil_ctest_cflags} (the recon's
# flags, -std=gnu17 -w: no -ffreestanding, because several tests rely on
# main's implicit 'return 0'; for libc.txt followed by %{msil_hosted_cflags})
# and %{msil_ctest_run}: %{msil_prog}, which builds the test through the
# leg's pipeline at each of its levels and runs it against
# %t/<name>.expected (as the native oracle build), the file the format
# writes from <name>.c.expected and '[exit 0]'; for a 'native <triple>'
# entry on that triple the native oracle's run that writes the file first,
# then %{msil_prog}; for a 'diag' entry one %{msil_prog_fail_<L>} per level,
# piped into FileCheck of the text (diag.check). The placeholders are
# redefined here, not given as substitutions of the format, which lit would
# apply before lit.cfg.py's definitions (its %{msil_srcs} would then win
# inside %{msil_prog}). Plan 5.12.9, 5.17.14. This file is not a test
# itself.
# REQUIRES: msil-corpus-c-testsuite
# REDEFINE: %{msil_srcs} = %{msil_corpus}/%{msil_ctest_tree}/tests/single-exec/%{msil_ctest_name}.c
# REDEFINE: %{msil_cflags} = %{msil_ctest_cflags}
# REDEFINE: %{msil_expect} = %t/%{msil_ctest_name}
# RUN: %{msil_ctest_run}
